@* Introduction.

\ID{$Id$}

We define the various classes here and the methods to access the
components.  Each class has a two-letter abbreviation which is used to
prefix the accessor functions.  Each class has 5 standard methods:

|CL_args(inst)| is how to pass a instance of a class to a subroutine.
|CL_dummy(inst)| is the dummy argument for a subroutine.
|CL_common| will declare common variable for this class.
|CL_decl(inst)| is how to declare a member of a class in a subroutine.
|CL_copy(inst1,inst2)| is how to copy one instance of a class to
another instance of the same class.
|CL_check(inst)| will check the consistency of the instance.

The instance above is designated in one of two ways.

For some classes, the information is held in global common blocks.  In
this case, the instance is denoted by an integer variable which is a
pointer into the common block arrays.  Examples of these classes are
elements, species, and reactions.

For the other classes, the instance is tagged with an arbitrary string.
The various components are obtained by constructing variable names from
that string.

In both cases, the instance can be an array reference and an array of
instances can be constructed in a natural manner; e.g.,
|CL_args(inst[3])| or |CL_decl(inst[0:5])|.

These combine in a natural way.  Thus if we are dealing with flight |f|,
then |fl_current(f)| is the current particle being tracked.  Its species
is |pt_sp(fl_current(f))|.  The first component of this species is
|sp_el(pt_sp(fl_current(f)),1)|, and so on.

@m FILE 'classes.web'

@ Element class.  This defines elements.  Prefix is |el|.  The
identifier |element| is an integer variable.

|el_name(element)| returns the long name (length |el_name_len|).
|el_sy(element)| returns the symbolic name (length |el_sy_len|).
|el_z(element)| returns the charge number.
|el_m(element)| returns the mass number (in amu).

@ Species class.  Prefix is |sp|.  The identifier |species| is an
integer variable.

|sp_name(species)| returns the long name (length |sp_name_len|).
|sp_sy(species)| returns the symbolic name (length |sp_sy_len|).
|sp_z(species)| returns the charge number.
|sp_m(species)| returns the mass (in kg).
|sp_ncomp(species)| returns the number of components in a species
(maximum size |sp_ncomp_max|).
|sp_el(species,i)| returns the element for the $i$th component.
|sp_count(species,i)| returns the count of the $i$th component.

@ Particle class.  Prefix is |pt|.  The identifier |particle| is a
string tag.

|pt_sp(particle)| is the species of the particle.
|pt_test(particle)| is the test particle index.
|pt_t(particle)| is the time for this particle.
|pt_w(particle)| is the statistical weight of the particle.
|pt_v(particle)| is the velocity of the particle.
|pt_loc(particle)| is the location of the particle (see below).
|pt_type(particle)| is the type of tracking to be done for the particle.

@ Routines involving particles.

@ Location class.  Prefix is |lc|.  The identifier |location| is a
string tag.

|lc_x(location)| is the position.
|lc_cell(location)| is the cell number.
|lc_zone(location)| is the zone number.
|lc_face(location)| is the face of the cell if at a face (else 0).
|lc_cell_next(location)| is the next cell number.
|lc_zone_next(location)| is the next zone number.

@ Routines using locations.

|lc_set(x)| sets other fields given the position.

@ Flight class. Prefix is |fl|.  The identifier |flight| is a string tag.

|fl_number(flight)| is the flight number.
|fl_origin(flight)| is the particle initiating the flight.
|fl_rand(flight)| is the random number information for the flight.
|fl_stack(flight)| is the stack of particles being processed.
|fl_pointer(flight)| is the pointer to the current particle (maximum size
|fl_stack_max|).
|fl_current(flight)| is the current particle (same as
|fl_stack(flight)[fl_pointer(flight)]|).

@ Reaction class.  Prefix is |rc|.  The identifier |reaction| is an
integer variable.

|rc_name(reaction)| is the name of the reaction (length |rc_name_len|).
|rc_sy(reaction)| is the symbolic name of the reaction (length |rc_sy_len|).
|rc_reagent_num(reaction)| is the number of reagents in the reaction
(maximum value |rc_reagent_max|).
|rc_reagent(reaction,i)| is the $i$th species reagent in the reaction.
|rc_product_num(reaction)| is the number of products (maximum value
|rc_product_max|).
|rc_product(reaction,i)| is the species of the $i$th product.

@ Materials class. Prefix is |ma|. The identifier |material| is an integer
variable. The properties of the materials are implicitly defined through
the physical behavior of the plasma-material interactions. As a result,
this class is really just an arbitary label; a given instance of the
class is generated only in conjunction with one or more PMI which refer
to it.

\begin{description}
  \item[|ma_num|] Number of material instances.
  \item[|ma_name(material)|] Returns the long name (length |ma_name_len|).
  \item[|ma_sy(material)|] Returns the symbolic name (length |ma_sy_len|).
\end{description}

@ Plasma-Material Interaction (PMI) class. Prefix is |pm|. 
The identifier |pmi| is
an integer variable.

\begin{description}
  \item[|pm_num|] Number of PMI in the reference database.
  \item[|pm_name(pmi)|] Returns the long name (length |pm_name_len|).
  \item[|pm_pmi_type(pmi)|] Returns the type of plasma-material interaction 
(length |pm_type_len|).
  \item[|pm_sy(pmi)|] Returns the symbolic name (length |pm_sy_len|).
  \item[|pm_reagent(pmi)|] Returns the species index (class |sp|)
of the incident projectile.
  \item[|pm_materials(pmi)|] Returns the materials index (class |ma|) of the 
target material.
  \item[|pm_gen(pmi)|] Contains the value |pmi_generic_yes| if this |pmi|
(and its data) can be applied to species
isotopically equivalent to |pm_reagent(pmi)|; the value |pmi_generic_no|
indicates that the |pmi| is species-specific.
  \item[|pm_product_num(pmi)|] Number of product particles.
  \item[|pm_product(pmi,i)|] Species index (class |sp|) of the |i|th product
particle, |i|$ = 1 \rightarrow$ |pm_product_num(pmi)|.
  \item[|pm_filename(pmi)|] Name of netCDF file containing the data 
(class |pf|) for this |pmi|.
\end{description}

@ Background class.  Prefix is |bk|.  The identifier |background| is an
integer variable.

|bk_sp(background)| is the species of the background.
|bk_n(background,i)| is the density in the $i$th zone.
|bk_v(background,i)| is the velocity in the $i$th zone.
|bk_temp(background,i)| is the temperature in the $i$th zone.

@ Zone class.  Prefix is |zn|.  The identifier |zone| is an integer variable.

|zn_type(zone)| is one of |zn_vacuum|, |zn_plasma|, |zn_solid|
indicating the type of zone.
|zn_index(zone,l)| for $l=1,$|zn_index_max| gives the fluid code
indices of the zone.
|zn_pointer(zone)| is a pointer into arrays specific for different types
of zones: plasma, solid, etc.
|zn_volume(zone)| is the volume of the zone.

@ Sector class. Prefix is |sc|.
A stratum
is identified by a positive integer |sector|.  This
gives information about the properties of the sector.  Each
sector is defined by a zone and a (directed) surface number. The array
providing the map from surface indices back to the sector indices
is held within the sector common
block.  

A set of sector subclasses are defined here as well for the purpose of
providing clearly defined arrays for storing sector-related properties.
With the exception of the ``diagnostic'' subclass, the subclass type
is the same as the sector's zone type. However, during tracking the stratum
type is used to infer the sector subclass since it is based on the zone 
type and also indicates whether or not the sector is of ``diagnostic''
type. The intention is that the number of these subclasses will change
infrequently; with new properties added occasionally. Hence, we have
opted, for clarity, to make the subclass and property names explicit
rather than store all of this information in some flexible array
structure.

\begin{description}
  \item[|nsectors|] Total number of sectors in the problem.
  \item[|strata[sector]|] Index of the stratum (class |sr|) to which |sector|
belongs.
  \item[|sectors[pointer]|] Array pointed to by |surface_sectors| (see
|geometry.web|) via the integer index |pointer|. The array |sectors| provides
the sector(s) index |sector| associated with a given surface. Need to use a
pointer here rather than a direct array with a |surface| index since there
may indeed be more than one |sector| associated with a given |surface|.
Nonetheless, because of the fact that a sector is defined by a single
(and only one) surface, the number of elements in |sectors| is just
|nsectors|.
  \item[|sector_zone[sector]|] Returns the zone (class |zn|)
associated with |sector|.
  \item[|sector_surface[sector]|] Returns the surface (see |geomint.hweb|)
associated with |sector|.
  \item[|sector_points|[sector][end][i]] Returns the
|i|th component of the position
vector at one |end| (i.e., |end| = 0 or 1) of |sector|.
  \item[|sc_vacuum_num|] Number of sectors in the vacuum subclass.
  \item[|sector_vacuum[sector]|] Returns the integer index into the |vacuum|
property arrays for |sector|; returns 0 if |sector| is not of vacuum type.
  \item[|sc_plasma_num|] Number of sectors in the plasma subclass.
  \item[|sector_plasma[sector]|] Returns the integer index into the |plasma|
property arrays for |sector|; returns 0 if |sector| is not of plasma type.
  \item[|sc_solid_num|] Number of sectors in the solid subclass.
  \item[|sector_solid[sector]|] Returns the integer index into the |solid|
property arrays for |sector|; returns 0 if |sector| is not of solid type.
  \item[|sc_solid_mat(solid)|] Material (class |ma|)
of the |solid|th solid-sector.
  \item[|sc_solid_temp(solid)|] Floating point temperature (in eV) of the 
|solid|th solid-sector.
  \item[|sc_exit_num|] Number of sectors in the exit (includes pump) subclass.
  \item[|sector_exit[sector]|] Returns the integer index into the |exit|
property arrays for |sector|; returns 0 if |sector| is not of exit type.
\end{description}

@ Strata class. Prefix is |sr|. A stratum
is identified by a positive integer |strata|.  This
gives information about the properties of the stratum.  Information
about the location of the strata is held within the sector common
block.  Each stratum is made up of the union of one or more sectors,
with a sector defined by a zone and a surface number.  This defines
a directed surface element; a stratum may include one or both directions.
Each sector may belong only to one stratum.  (We may want to relax
this restriction at some point.)
A rudimentary procedure for automatically defining strata (and sectors) has
been incorporated into |readgeometry.web|. As presently configured, the
sectors on either side of a two-sided stratum must have identical
end points. This is not a limitation on the main code, but just on the
present user interface.

The initial need for strata (and sectors) is as a place to store information
about materials; more generally they may be viewed as boundary conditions. 
Another potential use is as a surface diagnostic.

\begin{description}
  \item[|sr_num|] Total number of strata in the problem.
  \item[|sr_type(strata)|] Returns an integer denoting the stratum type (e.g.,
|sr_wall|, |sr_pump|, etc.).
  \item[|strata_type_num[i]|] Number of strata with |sr_type| = |i|.
\end{description}

@ Random class.  Prefix is |rn|.  The identifier |random| is a string.

|rn_index(random)| index to next random number.
|rn_max(random)| maximum index computed.
|rn_count(random)| count of random numbers computed.
|rn_array(random)| array of random numbers.
|rn_gauss_index(random)| index to next gaussian random number.
|rn_gauss_array(random)| array of gaussian random numbers.
|rn_iso_index(random)| index to next isotropically distributed vectors.
|rn_iso_array(random)| array of isotropically distributed vectors.
|rn_cos_index(random)| index to next cosine distributed vectors.
|rn_cos_array(random)| array of cosine distributed vectors.

@ Routines using the random numbers.

|rn_init(seed,random)| initialize random numbers from 2-element integer array
|seed|.

|rn_next(x,random)| next random number into $x$ (inline available).

|rn_fast_next(x,random)| fast version of this (inline available).

|rn_check(n,random)| check |n| numbers available for |rn_fast_next|.

|rn_gauss_next(x,random)| next gaussian number in $x$ (inline availble).

|rn_iso_next(v,random)| next isotropic vector number into $\vect v$
(inline availble).

|rn_cos_next(v,random)| next cosine vector number into $\vect v$
(inline availble).

|call random_seed(seed)| initialize seed from current time, etc.

|cell next_seed(n,seed,newseed)| advance |seed| forward $n$ steps.

@ Test particle class.  Prefix is |ts|.  Identifier |test| is an integer
index.

|ts_sp(test)| is the species of the test particle.
|ts_rc_num(test)| is the number of reactions this particle has.
|ts_rc(test,i)| is the number of the $i$th reaction.
|ts_tg(test,i)| is the target number for the $i$th reaction.
|ts_bk(test,i)| is the background number for the $i$th reaction.

@ Cross section class. Prefix is |xs|.

The properties associated with ``cross sections'' (which is a generic
designation for not only cross sections, but all related quantities
such as reaction rates, momentum transfer rates; the somewhat disjoint
set of parameters associated with elastic collisions will be included
as well) are presented below. Here, the subscript |j| denotes the index
for dependent variables (dimension variable |dep_var_ind|), while 
the nonzero values of the subscript |i| represent the independent
variables (dimension variables |rank_ind| and 
|rank_ind0|) needed to determine a particular value of variable |j|.
Some of these variables apply exclusively to ``fits'', some to
``tables''; some apply to both. 
\begin{description}
  \item[|xs_name|] String naming the ``cross section''. 
Same as the reaction name; that is, it should match
|rc_sy(rc_num)|.
  \item[|xs_num_dep_var|] Number of dependent variables associated with 
reaction |xs_name|.
  \item[|xs_organization[j]|] String describing the manner in which the 
data are organized. Examples
envisioned thus far are ``table'' and ``fit''.
  \item[|xs_rank[j]|] Integer number of independent variables for this particular
``cross section''.
  \item[|xs_var[0,j]|] String which tells more precisely what this quantity
is; e.g., `rate', `cross\_section'.
  \item[|xs_spacing[0,j]|] String describing the way the data are to be
interpolated. Examples are `linear' and `log'; applies to tabular organization
only.
  \item[|xs_units[0,j]|] String providing the units for this quantity; 
may be in any system.
  \item[|xs_mult[0,j]|] Floating point multiplier for this quantity. While
it may be used for any necessary purpose, it should provide the conversion
factor from |xs_units[0,j]| to the MKS system.
  \item[|xs_var[i,j]|] String describing independent variable |i|. Examples
contemplated thus far are `density', `energy', 'temperature', 
`specific\_energy' (i.e., energy per amu), and `specific\_temperature'. Note:
the ``non-specific'' forms should only be used for electron-impact processes
where there can be no confusion as to the relevant mass.
  \item[|xs_spacing[i,j]|] String telling the method of interpolation to be
used for independent variable |i|; applies to tabular organization only.
  \item[|xs_units[i,j]|] String providing the units for independent variable
|i|.
  \item[|xs_mult[i,j]|] Floating point multipier for independent variable |i|.
It should provide the conversion factor from |xs_units[i]| to the MKS system.
  \item[|xs_tab_index[i,j]|] For tabular organizations, 
this integer provides the
number of values used for independent variable |i| and dependent
variable |j|. 
  \item[|xs_min[i,j]|] Floating point minimum value to be used for independent
variable |i| in a table. It will be combined with the maximum and the number
of values to actually define the array of independent variable values. For a
fit, provides the lower range of validity for this independent variable.
  \item[|xs_max[i,j]|] Floating point maximum value to be used for independent
variable |i| in a table. For a
fit, provides the upper range of validity for this independent variable.
  \item[|xs_table1|] Table of values for the first dependent variable. Analogous
variables are defined for each of the other |j| dependent variables. The
(nontrivial) ranks of these table are |xs_rank[j]|. For a fit, this variable
contains the fitting coefficients. In this case, |xs_table| will effectively
be of rank one even if $|xs_rank| > 1$.
  \item[|xs_eval_name[j]|] Name of routine used to evaluate fit for dependent
variable |j|; set to ``unknown'' for tabular organization.
\end{description}
Ideally |xs_table| would have an additional index associated with
   the number of dependent variables. However, since the rank and size
   of the table is in general different for each dependent variable, it
   is more efficient to define a separate table for each dependent variable;
   i.e., to make the dependent variable number explicitly part of the
   name. The variable dimensioning is then accomplished with the
   |indexivj_ind| dimensions defined above. It would be convenient if
   these could be some sort of array, but since they eventually appear
   as dimensions for netCDF, that is not possible. Furthermore, note that
   in these two sets of variables, |xs_tablej| and |indexivj_ind|, the
   values of the parameters |xs_dep_var_max| (corresponds to |j|) and
   |xs_table_rank_max| (corresponds to |i|) are {\em hardwired}. If these
   parameters ever need to be changed, the {\em number} of these 
   variables needs to altered accordingly.

@ Rate calculation class. Prefix is |ra|

The variables in this class specify the information required to retrieve
data from external database files. Presently, the database is assumed
to be ALADDIN. The specific variables used here reflect that assumption.
A single file name and set of hierarchical search labels are used for
all of the data to be associated with one particular instance of this
class. However, as many as |xs_dep_var_max| different (index |j| below)
sets of Boolean search labels may be specified. This allows different
quantities (e.g., cross sections and reaction rates) to be included here,
but forces them to be closely related. In this way, we hope to ensure
overall consistency of the data.

\begin{description}
  \item[|ra_data_filename|] File name from which data will be extracted.
  \item[|ra_mass|] Mass to be used in relating energy and velocity when
evaluating the argument of the function specified by this instance.
  \item[|ra_mass_units|] Units of |ra_mass|.
  \item[|ra_hier_label|] Array of of hierarchical search labels used to
identify (e.g., for use with ALADDIN) data. 
  \item[|ra_num_hier|] Number of search labels in array |ra_hier_label|.
  \item[|ra_bool_and_label[j]|] Array of labels used to further identify
information associated with dependent variable |j|; in searching the data
files, entries which {\em contain} these strings are selected.
  \item[|ra_num_bool_and[j]|] Number of labels in |ra_bool_and_label|.
  \item[|ra_bool_not_label[j]|] Array of labels used to further identify
information assciated with dependent variable |j|; in searching the data files,
entries which {\em do not contain} these strings are selected.
  \item[|ra_num_bool_not[j]|] Number of labels in |ra_bool_not_label|.
\end{description}

@ PMI format class. Prefix is |pf|.

This class provides a format for external storage of data which deal
with PMI. Here, the subscript |j| denotes the index
for dependent variables, while 
the nonzero values of the subscript |i| represent the independent
variables needed to determine a particular value of variable |j|.

\begin{description}
  \item[|pf_name|] String (length |pm_sy_len| naming the PMI.
Same as the name of the PMI in the |pm| class; that is, it should match
|pm_sy|.
  \item[|pf_min[j][i]|] Floating point minimum value to be used for the 
|i|th independent variable of the |j|the dependent variable.
It will be combined with the maximum and the number
of values to actually define the array of independent variable values.
  \item[|pf_max[j][i]|] Floating point maximum value to be used for 
the |i|th independent of the |j|th depdendent variable.
  \item[|pf_mult[j][i]|] Floating point multipier for the |i|th
independent variable of the |j|th dependent variable. |i| = 0 refers
to the dependent variable itself.
It should provide the conversion factor from |pf_units[j][i]| to the 
MKS system.
  \item[|pf_num_dep_var|] Number of dependent variables associated with 
PMI |pf_name|.
  \item[|pf_rank[j]|] Integer number of independent variables for 
the |j|th dependent variable.
  \item[|pf_spacing[j][i]|] String (length |pf_tag_string_length|
describing the way the data are to be
interpolated. Examples are `linear', `log', and 'nonuniform'; 
applies to tabular data only.
  \item[|pf_var[j][i]|] String (length |pf_tag_string_length|)
describing the |i|th independent variable of the |j|th dependent variable.
|i| = 0 refers to the dependent variable itself.
Examples are 'yield', 'energy\_in', etc.; see |pmiformat.hweb| for a
complete list.
  \item[|pf_units[j][i]|] String (length |pf_eval_string_length|) 
providing the units for the |i|th independent variable
|i|. String providing the units for this quantity; 
may be in any system.
  \item[|pf_eval_name[j]|] String 
(length |pf_eval_string_length|) identifying the means for
evaluating the |j|th dependent variable. 
The most prevalent value is ``table''; other
values must correspond to the name of a fitting subroutine. See 
|plate.web| and the description of the |pd| class
for more details. Note that in some instances, the appropriate
dependent ``variable'' is really a vector (i.e., velocity); in this case,
tabular evaluation (which can return only a single scalar) makes no sense.
factor from |pf_units[0,j]| to the MKS system.
  \item[|pf_tab_index[j][i]|] Integer
number of values used for the |i|th independent variable of the |j|th 
dependent variable.
  \item[|pf_indep_var_base[j][i]|] Index in 
|pf_indep_var_tab| at which the |i|th independent variable values for the
|j|th variable begin. 
This array is set by the macros associated with
|pf_ragged_alloc| and |pf_ragged_realloc|.
  \item[|pf_indep_var_tab[macro_index]|] Is the actual object which
holds the values of the independent variables.
This is a
1-D array; its size is |pf_indep_var_size|, an integer variable
set by the macros |pf_ragged_alloc| and |pf_ragged_realloc|.
  \item[|pf_indep_var_table(h,i,j)|] The programmer's 
representation of the independent variable values for the |i|th rank of the 
|j|th dependent variable for |pr_pmi|.
Note that |h| = 0 $\rightarrow$ |pf_tab_index[j][i]| - 1.
  \item[|pf_data_base[j]|] Index in |pf_data_tab|
at which the data for the |j|th dependent variable begin.
This array is set by the macros associated with |pf_ragged_alloc| and
|pf_ragged_realloc|.
  \item[|pf_data_inc[j]|] Is the number of elements in |pf_data_table|
required for the |j|th dependent variable. 
This array is set by the macros associated with |pf_ragged_alloc| and
|pf_ragged_realloc|.
  \item[|pf_data_tab[macro_index]|] is the actual data object which
holds the data. As described above, this
is a 1-D array; its size is |pf_data_size|, an integer variable set
by the macros |pf_ragged_alloc| and |pf_ragged_realloc|.
  \item[|pd_handling(k,l,m,n,o,j)|] The programmer's 
representation of the handling data.
|k|, |l|, |m|, |n|, and |o| represent the up-to-five
dimensional dependency of the |j|th dependent variable on its 
independent variables.
|k| ranges from 0 $\rightarrow$ |pf_tab_index[j][1]| - 1;
similarly for |l| with |i|=2, |m|, |i|=3, etc.
\end{description}

@ Problem class. Prefix is |pr|. 

These properties characterize the
interactions between the various aspects of the problem: test particles,
background species, reactions, materials, and plasma-material interactions.
The integer indices and array values
noted below correspond
to entries into one of several lists.
For clarity, we will associate with each list a different subscript to
be used in the subsequent definitions.
These are:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Reference list of species (input, class |sp|) (subscript: $s$),
  \item Reference list of reactions (input, class |rc|) (subscript: $r$),
  \item List of test species (problem input) (subscript: $t$),
  \item List of background species (problem input) (subscript: $b$),
  \item List of problem reactions (problem input) (subscript: $pr$),
  \item Reference list of materials (input, class |ma|) (subscript: $m$),
  \item List of problem materials (subset of the reference list, specified
in problem input) (subscript: $ms$),
  \item Reference list of plasma-materials interactions (input, class |pm|) 
(subscript: $p$),
  \item List of problem PMI (subset of the reference list, specified
in problem input) (subscript: $ps$),
\end{enumerate}
The index $i$ below refers to an element of each of these lists. Once we've got
the subset lists specified in the ``problem input'', we can identify the
reactions and PMI in which particular test species participates. Indices
for these sub-subset lists are denoted by $j$ below.

The common variables are:
\begin{description}
  \item{|pr_test_num|} Number of test species in the problem.
  \item{|pr_background_num|} Number of background species in the problem.
  \item{|pr_reaction_num|} Number of reactions in the problem.
  \item{|pr_materials_num|} Number of materials in the problem.
  \item{|pr_pmi_num|} Number of plasma-material interactions in the problem.
  \item{|pr_test|($i_{t}$)} Species index of test species $i_{t}$.
  \item{|problem_species_test|[$i_{s}$]} Test index of reference species 
$i_{s}$.
  \item{|pr_background|($i_{b}$)} Species index of background species $i_{b}$.
  \item{|problem_species_background|[$i_{s}$]} Background index of reference 
species $i_{s}$.
  \item{|pr_mat_ref|($i_{ms}$)} Reference materials index of the $i_{ms}$ 
entry in the problem materials subset.
  \item{|pr_materials_sub|[$i_{m}$]} Problem index of reference material
$i_{m}$.
  \item{|pr_reaction|($i_{pr}$)} Index into the reference list of reactions for
problem reaction $i_{pr}$.
  \item{|pr_rc_num|($i_{t}$)} Number of reactions in which test species 
$i_{t}$ participates.
  \item{|pr_ts_rc|($i_{t}$,$j_{pr}$)} Index of the problem (subset) reaction
for the $j_{pr}$th reaction involving test species $i_{t}$,$j_{pr} = 1 
\rightarrow |pr_rc_num|(i_{t})$.
  \item{|pr_ts_bk|($i_{t}$,$j_{pr}$)} Index into the list of (problem) 
background species for the $j_{pr}$th reaction involving test species $i_{t}$.
  \item{|pr_num_arrangements|($i_{t}$,$j_{pr}$)} Number of arrangements of
products (which may or may not be truly different) for  the $j_{pr}$th 
reaction involving test species $i_{t}$.
  \item{|pr_ts_prod|($i_{t}$,$j_{pr}$,$k$,$l$)} The $l$th product (equivalent
to the $l$th generic species in |rc_product|) for the $k$th arrangement of
the $j_{pr}$th reaction involving test species $i_{t}$.
  \item{|pr_prod_mult|($i_{t}$,$j_{pr}$,$k$)} Relative weight or 
multiplicity of the $k$th
arrangement of  the $j_{pr}$th reaction involving test species $i_{t}$. This
quantity is the probability of obtaining arrangement $k$ in this reaction.
  \item{|pr_pm_ref|($i_{ps}$)} Index into the reference list of PMI for
problem (subset) PMI $i_{ps}$.
  \item{|problem_pmi_sub|[$i_{p}$]} Index of the problem (subset) PMI 
corresponding to reference PMI $i_{p}$.
  \item{|pr_pm_case_num|($i_{t}$)} Number of PMI in which test species 
$i_{t}$ participates.
  \item{|pr_pm_cases|($i_{t}$,$j_{ps}$)} Index of the problem (subset) PMI
for the $j_{ps}$th reaction involving test species $i_{t}$, $j_{ps} = 1
\rightarrow |pr_pm_case_num|(i_{t})$.

  \item{|pr_pm_num_arrange|($i_{t}$,$j_{ps}$)} Number of arrangements of
products (which may or may not be truly different) for  the $j_{ps}$th 
PMI involving test species $i_{t}$.
  \item{|pr_pm_prod|($i_{t}$,$j_{ps}$,$k$,$l$)} The $l$th product 
for the $k$th arrangement of
the $j_{ps}$th PMI involving test species $i_{t}$.
  \item{|pr_prod_mult|($i_{t}$,$j_{ps}$,$k$)} Relative weight or 
multiplicity of the $k$th
arrangement of  the $j_{ps}$th PMI involving test species $i_{t}$. This
quantity is the probability of obtaining arrangement $k$ in this PMI.

\end{description}

@ Reaction Data class. Prefix is |rd|. 

A given reaction is identified
by an integer index |pr_reac| (``problem reaction'').
This is the internal class corresponding to the 
``cross section'' class |xs|. It contains all of the data required to
deal with the reactions in the problem. These arrays are basically broken
into two very similar subclasses. The first has names which begin with
|reaction_data|; these arrays are used only to contain the reaction
rate and cross section (when needed) information for a particular |pr_reac|.
The second subclass has names starting with |reaction_misc| and is used
for just about everything else. Namely, it will contain the genuinely
miscellaneous data needed to process a particular reaction. The nature
and name of these data objects can vary not only with the reaction, but
with the model used to represent a given reaction.

The representation of the data in memory is crucial to efficiency. Since the
main data tables here are five dimensional objects of very ``ragged''
structure, we have opted for an internal representation as single dimensional
arrays. To simplify coding, macros have been developed to simulate the
familiar multi-dimensional array functionality. The downside of this
is the addition of new bookkeeping arrays (here, the |data_base| arrays),
the unwieldy-ness of the macros themselves, and the difficulty in extracting
data from the 1-D array during debugging or while perusing the netCDF files.
At least this last issue may be addressed by the construction of a small
browser code to allow multi-dimensional netCDF data to be viewed and 
plotted in a straightforward manner. The macros used in |pmidata.hweb| are
more general than these; an attempt at documenting their operation can be
found in that file.

\begin{description}
  \item[|reaction_organization[pr_reac][j]|] Integer identifying the manner
in which the data for the |j|th dependent variable of |pr_reac| arranged;
examples are |xs_organization_fit| and |xs_organization_table|.
  \item[|reaction_data_min[pr_reac][j][i]|] Floating point minimum value of
the |i|th independent variable for the |j|th dependent variable in |pr_reac|.
  \item[|reaction_data_delta[pr_reac][j][i]|] Floating point increment in
the |i|th independent variable for the |j|th dependent variable in |pr_reac|.
  \item[|reaction_data_rank[pr_reac][j]|] Integer number of independent
variables for the |j|th dependent variable in |pr_reac|.
  \item[|reaction_data_spacing[pr_reac][j][i]|] Integer identifying the way
the |i|th variable for the |j|th dependent variable (|i| = 0 refers to the
dependent variable itself) are to be interpolated. Examples are
|xs_spacing_linear| and |xs_spacing_log|. Namely, in the latter case the
log of the independent variable (or dependent if |i| = 0) is interpolated
linearly.
  \item[|reaction_data_tab_index[pr_reac][j][i]|] Number of values used for
the |i|th independent variable of the |j|th dependent variable.
  \item[|reaction_data_base[pr_reac][j]|] Index in |reaction_data_table|
at which the data for the |j|th dependent variable of |pr_reac| begin.
This array is set by the macros associated with |rd_ragged_alloc| and
|rd_ragged_realloc|.
  \item[|reaction_data_var[pr_reac][j][i]|] Integer identifying the |i|th
independent variable for the |j|th dependent variable. Again, |i| = 0
refers to the dependent variable itself; only the reaction rate
|xs_var_rate| or cross section |xs_var_cross_section| are included here.
For definiteness, the reaction rate data are assigned |j| = |rd_rate| and
cross section data |j| = |rd_xs| in the |reaction_data| arrays. The 
independent variables are typically temperature, density, or energy; see
|xsection.hweb| for a full list.
  \item[|reaction_data_table[macro_index]|] is the actual data object which
holds the reaction rate and cross section data. As described above, this
is a 1-D array; its size is |reaction_data_size|, an integer variable set
by the macros |rd_ragged_alloc| and |rd_ragged_realloc|.
  \item[|rd_data3(pr_reac,j,k,l,m)|] The programmer's representation of the
reaction rate and cross section data for |pr_reac|. As above, |j| denotes
the dependent variable. Now, |k|, |l|, and |m| represent the up-to-three
dimensional dependency of that variable on its independent variables.
|k| ranges from 0 $\rightarrow$ |reaction_data_tab_index[pr_reac][j][1]| - 1;
similarly for |l| with |i|=2, and |m|, |i|=3.
  \item[|reaction_misc_organization[pr_reac][j]|] Integer identifying the 
manner in which the data for the |j|th dependent variable of |pr_reac| 
arranged; examples are |xs_organization_fit| and |xs_organization_table|.
  \item[|reaction_misc_min[pr_reac][j][i]|] Floating point minimum value of
the |i|th independent variable for the |j|th dependent variable in |pr_reac|.
  \item[|reaction_misc_delta[pr_reac][j][i]|] Floating point increment in
the |i|th independent variable for the |j|th dependent variable in |pr_reac|.
  \item[|reaction_misc_rank[pr_reac][j]|] Integer number of independent
variables for the |j|th dependent variable in |pr_reac|.
  \item[|reaction_misc_spacing[pr_reac][j][i]|] Integer identifying the way
the |i|th variable for the |j|th dependent variable (|i| = 0 refers to the
dependent variable itself) are to be interpolated. Examples are
|xs_spacing_linear| and |xs_spacing_log|. Namely, in the latter case the
log of the independent variable (or dependent if |i| = 0) is interpolated
linearly.
  \item[|reaction_misc_tab_index[pr_reac][j][i]|] Number of values used for
the |i|th independent variable of the |j|th dependent variable.
  \item[|reaction_misc_base[pr_reac][j]|] Index in |reaction_misc_table|
at which the data for the |j|th dependent variable of |pr_reac| begin.
This array is set by the macros associated with |rd_ragged_alloc| and
|rd_ragged_realloc|.
  \item[|reaction_misc_var_dep[pr_reac][j]|] String 
(length |xs_tag_string_length|) identifying the |j|th dependent variable. 
A string is used here since the nature of the miscellaneous data varies
from one reaction to the next.
  \item[|reaction_misc_var_indep[pr_reac][j][i]|] Integer identifying the
|i|th independent variable for the |j|th dependent variable. The
independent variables are typically temperature, density, or energy; see
|xsection.hweb| for a full list.
  \item[|reaction_misc_table[macro_index]|] is the actual data object which
holds the miscellaneous data. As described above, this
is a 1-D array; its size is |reaction_misc_size|, an integer variable set
by the macros |rd_ragged_alloc| and |rd_ragged_realloc|.
  \item[|rd_misc_data3(pr_reac,j,k,l,m)|] The programmer's 
representation of the miscellaneous data for |pr_reac|. As above, |j| denotes
the dependent variable. Now, |k|, |l|, and |m| represent the up-to-three
dimensional dependency of that variable on its independent variables.
|k| ranges from 0 $\rightarrow$ |reaction_misc_tab_index[pr_reac][j][1]| - 1;
similarly for |l| with |i|=2, and |m|, |i|=3.
\end{description}

@ PMI data class. Prefix is |pd|.

A given PMI is identified by an integer index |pr_pmi| (``problem PMI''). This
is the internal class corresponding to the ``PMI format'' class |pf|. It 
contains all of the data required to deal with the PMI in the problem. Not
by accident, the elements of this class closely resemble those of the
reaction data class |rd|. Nonetheless, there are some significant 
differences. The biggest of these is the allowance for non-uniformly spaced
independent variables. The consequent burden is the addition of a new
array to store all of the independent variables. Like the main data
table (see the above discussion on the |rd| class), 
this is a ragged array and is stored in a 1-D array, but represented
as a multi-dimensional object through macros. Also, data ``fits'' as well
as tables are being used to evaluate the data in this class. As a result
of this practical experience, the class variables have been massaged 
somewhat to improve the similarities between the two treatments. The
hope is that the reaction data will be refitted to provide the same 
functionality.

For clarity, here are the assumptions made about fit data:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item |rank| provides the number of independent variables required to
evaluate the fit; their identity is stored in the corresponding |var| array
just as for tables.
  \item The data table passed is considered to be strictly 1-D with 
|tab_index[1]| elements.
  \item No spacing or value information is stored for the independent 
variables. Since this information is at least 
likely to be peculiar to each fit and at most irrelevant, it is assumed to
reside within the fit function.
  \item Eventually, we would like to store the fit function along with the
data so that the data could be completely described by a single package,
as can now be done with tables.
\end{enumerate}

The PMI data class is divided into two similar subclasses. The first 
represents a single dependent variable, the ``yield'' of the PMI (e.g.,
the reflection coefficient, adsorption rate, sputtering yield, etc.; 
in effect, the probability of the process taking place). Hence, this
sub-class lacks any explicit ``dependent variable'' dimension in the arrays.
The |pmi_handling| arrays contain the data required to process or ``handle''
|pr_pmi|.

\begin{description}
  \item[|pmi_yield_min[pr_pmi][i]|] Floating point minimum value of
the |i|th independent variable of the yield in |pr_pmi|.
  \item[|pmi_yield_delta[pr_pmi][i]|] Floating point increment in
the |i|th independent variable of the yield in |pr_pmi|.
  \item[|pmi_yield_rank[pr_pmi]|] Integer number of independent
variables for the yield in |pr_pmi|.
  \item[|pmi_yield_spacing[pr_pmi][i]|] Integer identifying the way
the |i|th variable for the yield (|i| = 0 refers to the
yield itself) are to be interpolated. Examples are
|pf_spacing_linear|, |pf_spacing_log|, and |pf_spacing_nonuniform|.
 Namely, in the log case the
log of the independent variable (or dependent if |i| = 0) is interpolated
linearly. In the nonuniform case, the independent variable values are
stored in detail.
  \item[|pmi_yield_eval_name[pr_pmi]|] String 
(length |pf_eval_string_length|) identifying the means for
evaluating the yield data. The most prevalent value is ``table''; other
values must correspond to the name of a fitting subroutine. See 
|plate.web| for more details.
  \item[|pmi_yield_tab_index[pr_pmi][i]|] Number of values used for
the |i|th independent variable of the yield.
  \item[|pmi_yield_var[pr_pmi][i]|] Integer identifying the |i|th
independent variable for the yield. The |i| = 0 entry is redundant and
should always have the value |pf_var_yield|. The 
independent variables are typically incident energy, and incident 
poloidal angle; see |pmiformat.hweb| for a full list.
  \item[|pmi_yield_num_random[pr_pmi]|] Integer number of uniform 
random variates
required to evaluate the yield for a single interaction of |pr_pmi|.
This is provided essentially for compatibility with the |pmi_handling|
variables.
  \item[|pmi_yield_base[pr_pmi]|] Index in |pmi_yield_table|
at which the data for the yield of |pr_pmi| begin.
This array is set by the macros associated with |pd_ragged_alloc| and
|pd_ragged_realloc|.
  \item[|pmi_yield_tab[macro_index]|] Is the actual data object which
holds the yield data. As described above, this
is a 1-D array; its size is |pmi_yield_size|, an integer variable set
by the macros |pd_ragged_alloc| and |pd_ragged_realloc|.
  \item[|pd_yield(k,l,m,n,o,pr_pmi)|] The programmer's representation of the
yield data for |pr_pmi|. |k|, |l|, |m|, |n|, and |o| represent the up-to-five
dimensional dependency of the yield on its independent variables.
|k| ranges from 0 $\rightarrow$ |pmi_yield_tab_index[pr_pmi][1]| - 1;
similarly for |l| with |i|=2, |m|, |i|=3, etc.
  \item[|pmi_yield_indep_var_base[pr_pmi][i]|] Index in 
|pmi_yield_indep_var_tab| at which the independent variable values for the
|i|th rank 
of the |pr_pmi| yield begin. This array is set by the macros associated with
|pd_ragged_alloc| and |pd_ragged_realloc|.
  \item[|pmi_yield_indep_var_tab[macro_index]|] Is the actual object which
holds the values of the independent variables for the yield. This is a
1-D array; its size is |pmi_yield_indep_var_size|, an integer variable
set by the macros |pd_ragged_alloc| and |pd_ragged_realloc|.
  \item[|pd_yield_indep_var(h,i,pr_pmi)|] The programmer's representation of
the independent variable values for the |i|th rank of the yield for |pr_pmi|.
Note that |h| = 0 $\rightarrow$ |pd_yield_tab_index[pr_pmi][i]| - 1.
  \item[|pmi_handling_min[pr_pmi][j][i]|] Floating point minimum value of
the |i|th independent variable for the |j|th dependent variable in |pr_pmi|.
  \item[|pmi_handling_delta[pr_pmi][j][i]|] Floating point increment in
the |i|th independent variable for the |j|th dependent variable in |pr_pmi|.
  \item[|pmi_handling_rank[pr_pmi][j]|] Integer number of independent
variables for the |j|th dependent variable in |pr_pmi|.
  \item[|pmi_handling_spacing[pr_pmi][j][i]|] Integer identifying the way
the |i|th variable for the |j|th dependent variable (|i| = 0 refers to the
dependent variable itself) are to be interpolated. Examples are
|pf_spacing_linear| and |pf_spacing_log|. Namely, in the log case the
log of the independent variable (or dependent if |i| = 0) is interpolated
linearly. In the nonuniform case, the independent variable values
are stored in detail.
  \item[|pmi_handling_eval_name[pr_pmi][j]|] String 
(length |pf_eval_string_length|) identifying the means for
evaluating the |j|th dependent variable. 
The most prevalent value is ``table''; other
values must correspond to the name of a fitting subroutine. See 
|plate.web| for more details. Note that in some instances, the appropriate
dependent ``variable'' is really a vector (i.e., velocity); in this case,
tabular evaluation (which can return only a single scalar) makes no sense.
  \item[|pmi_handling_tab_index[pr_pmi][j][i]|] Number of values used for
the |i|th independent variable of the |j|th dependent variable.
  \item[|pmi_handling_var0[pr_pmi][j]|] String 
(length |pf_tag_string_length|) identifying the |j|th dependent variable. 
A string is used here since the nature of the handling data varies
from one PMI to the next.
  \item[|pmi_handling_var[pr_pmi][j][i]|] Integer identifying the
|i|th independent variable for the |j|th dependent variable. 
The 
independent variables are typically incident energy, and incident 
poloidal angle; see |pmiformat.hweb| for a full list.
  \item[|pmi_handling_num_random[pr_pmi]|] Integer number of uniform 
random variates
required to handle a single interaction of |pr_pmi|.
The need for this became apparent when implementing the Bateman format.
In this case, the {\em same random number} is needed to evaluate more than
one dependent variable. |pmi_handling_num_random| lets the code 
maintain them through all of the required dependent variable evaluations.
  \item[|pmi_handling_base[pr_pmi][j]|] Index in |pmi_handling_table|
at which the data for the |j|th dependent variable of |pr_pmi| begin.
This array is set by the macros associated with |pd_ragged_alloc| and
|pd_ragged_realloc|.
  \item[|pmi_handling_tab[macro_index]|] is the actual data object which
holds the handling data. As described above, this
is a 1-D array; its size is |pmi_handling_size|, an integer variable set
by the macros |pd_ragged_alloc| and |pd_ragged_realloc|.
  \item[|pd_handling(k,l,m,n,o,j,pr_pmi)|] The programmer's 
representation of the handling data for |pr_pmi|. 
|k|, |l|, |m|, |n|, and |o| represent the up-to-five
dimensional dependency of the |j|th dependent variable on its 
independent variables.
|k| ranges from 0 $\rightarrow$ |pmi_handling_tab_index[pr_pmi][j][1]| - 1;
similarly for |l| with |i|=2, |m|, |i|=3, etc.
  \item[|pmi_handling_indep_var_base[pr_pmi][j][i]|] Index in 
|pmi_handling_indep_var_tab| at which the |i|th 
independent variable values for the
|j|th handling variable of |pr_pmi| begin. 
This array is set by the macros associated with
|pd_ragged_alloc| and |pd_ragged_realloc|.
  \item[|pmi_handling_indep_var_tab[macro_index]|] Is the actual object which
holds the values of the independent variables for the handling variables. 
This is a
1-D array; its size is |pmi_handling_indep_var_size|, an integer variable
set by the macros |pd_ragged_alloc| and |pd_ragged_realloc|.
  \item[|pd_handling_indep_var(h,i,j,pr_pmi)|] The programmer's 
representation of the independent variable values for the |i|th rank of the 
|j|th dependent variable for |pr_pmi|.
Note that |h| = 0 $\rightarrow$ |pd_handling_tab_index[pr_pmi][j][i]| - 1.
\end{description}

